    "knowledge_point": "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Irrational",
    "problem": "The equation ${y}^{2}+16=-12y$ can be written in the form $(y+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-12y$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+16 & =-12y \\\\ {y}^{2}+12y & =-16 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6\\cdot y & =-16 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6\\cdot y+{6}^{2}-{6}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ (y+6)^{2}-{6}^{2} & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+6)^{2}-{6}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ (y+6)^{2} & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=6,q=20,$ and $p+q=26.$",
